.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt NAME_TO_HANDLE_AT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm name_to_handle_at ,
.Nm open_by_handle_at
.Nd name a file by a handle, and open it by the handle
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn name_to_handle_at "int dirfd" "const char *path" "struct file_handle *handle" "int *mount_id" "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn open_by_handle_at "int mount_fd" "struct file_handle *handle" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn name_to_handle_at
writes into
.Fa handle
a handle that identifies the file
.Fa path
names within its mount, and into
.Fa mount_id
the id
.Pa /proc/PID/mountinfo
gives that mount, which
.Xr stat 2 Ns 's
.Fn statx
reports as
.Dv STATX_MNT_ID .
The path resolves as
.Fn fstatat
resolves it, from
.Fa dirfd ,
with a final symbolic link named itself unless
.Fa flags
has
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW ;
with
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
and an empty path, the file is what
.Fa dirfd
refers to.
.Pp
A handle is a
.Vt struct file_handle :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct file_handle {
    unsigned int  handle_bytes; /* the size of f_handle */
    int           handle_type;
    unsigned char f_handle[];
};
.Ed
.Pp
The caller sets
.Va handle_bytes
to the room after the two fields, at most
.Dv MAX_HANDLE_SZ ,
128.
Rach2's handles are Linux's
.Dv FILEID_INO64_GEN ,
type 0x51: twelve bytes, the file's inode number in 64 bits, least
significant byte first, and a generation of 0.
A
.Va handle_bytes
below 12 gets 12, and no handle, and the call fails with
.Er EOVERFLOW
after writing
.Fa mount_id ,
so that a caller can ask again with room, as on Linux.
.Pp
A handle of a file on ext2, tmpfs, vfat or KRFFS, the types Linux gives
export operations, and Rach2's own, reopens the file.
On any other mount,
.Pa /proc ,
.Pa /sys ,
.Pa /dev ,
exFAT, debugfs and the message queues among them, a handle only
identifies the file, and
.Fn name_to_handle_at
gives one only with
.Dv AT_HANDLE_FID
in
.Fa flags ,
as Linux gives a type without export operations.
libudev and systemd ask for a handle to learn a file's mount id, and
read the mount id from
.Pa /proc/self/fdinfo/N
when the call fails.
.Pp
.Fn open_by_handle_at
opens the file
.Fa handle
names on the mount of
.Fa mount_fd ,
any descriptor of a file of that mount, or the working directory for
.Dv AT_FDCWD .
.Fa flags
are those of
.Xr open 2 ,
less
.Dv O_CREAT
and
.Dv O_EXCL ,
and with
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW ,
so that a symbolic link opens only with
.Dv O_PATH .
The file is found by its inode on the mount, below the mount's root,
and opened by its present path, from the caller's root, with
.Xr open 2 Ns 's
checks; a file renamed since the handle was made is found under its new
name.
The caller must be privileged, as Linux asks
.Dv CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH .
.Ss System call interface
On i686 the calls are numbers 341 and 342, which the x86-64 kernel's
compat table gives an i386 program too, and on x86-64 numbers 303 and
304.
The structure has one layout on both, and both kernels take the handle
of either.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn name_to_handle_at
returns 0, and
.Fn open_by_handle_at
the new descriptor.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel calls return negative error numbers on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/statxmusl.c
asks for the handle of a file in
.Pa /tmp
with no room and then with room, compares the mount id with
.Pa /proc/self/mountinfo ,
asks for the handle of a
.Pa /proc
file with and without
.Dv AT_HANDLE_FID ,
and, as root, reopens the file by its handle and reads it.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EOPNOTSUPP]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa dirfd
or
.Fa mount_fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A pointer cannot be accessed.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
of
.Fn name_to_handle_at
has a bit other than
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW ,
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
and
.Dv AT_HANDLE_FID ;
.Va handle_bytes
is above
.Dv MAX_HANDLE_SZ ,
or 0 for
.Fn open_by_handle_at ;
or
.Va handle_type
is negative.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
A component of
.Fa path
does not exist.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel could not allocate a path page.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
The file's type reopens nothing and
.Dv AT_HANDLE_FID
was not given, or the object is on no mount of the table: a pipe, a
socket or an anonymous descriptor.
.It Bq Er EOVERFLOW
.Va handle_bytes
is below 12.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller of
.Fn open_by_handle_at
is not privileged.
.It Bq Er ESTALE
The handle is of another type or size, the mount's files do not reopen,
or no file of the mount has the handle's inode, or the file is outside
the caller's root.
.El
.Pp
.Fn open_by_handle_at
fails with the errors of
.Xr open 2
as well, and
.Fn name_to_handle_at
with those of
.Xr stat 2
for the path.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
